  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Although often called a "rivière" (stream), the Siagne is actually a river of about 42 km (according to the Pays de Fayence tourist office), rising at the foot of the Audibergue in the commune of Escragnolles and flowing into the Mediterranean at Mandelieu. It runs through 12 communes in the Var and the Alpes-Maritimes, including Mons, Callian, Montauroux and Tanneron. Its gorges, up to 400 m deep in places, form a Natura 2000 site with petrifying waterfalls, natural bridges and caves.</p>
      <p>The Canal de la Siagne, built to supply Grasse and Cannes with drinking water, runs alongside part of the course.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The Pays de Fayence tourist office states that fishing, hiking and swimming are possible, while asking visitors to protect this fragile site. It gives no rule on dogs; a Pays de Fayence guide points out that, unless a municipal order is posted, river banks allow dogs to swim. Keep your dog on a leash and look at the signs on-site. Access varies a lot depending on the stretch: some access routes are long and difficult (for example the hike to the Pont des Tuves, starting from the Montauroux stadium).</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Siagnole, a tributary of the Siagne near Mons, is a bit more secluded — see the dedicated listing for the Pont de Mons.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Lake Saint-Cassien, fed by a tributary of the Siagne, is nearby for a more classic lake swim; check the dog rules before going, though, as they differ from beach to beach.</p>
    </div>

